<article id="lab-article" aria-label="How CyberAlchemy Lab works">
<div class="introduction" id="foundations">
<p><strong>CyberAlchemy is a system for organizing knowledge and work.</strong> It is built on definitions because any careful explanation depends on what its terms mean. A definition states what a term means, the context in which that meaning applies and the boundaries it establishes.</p>
<p>In ordinary life, much of this remains unstated. Memory and accumulated experience make familiar meanings feel obvious. We speak of a successful project, correct software or confidence in a result without stopping to define every word. Shared understanding lets the conversation proceed. It also makes definitions easy to overlook.</p>
<p>Once that understanding must be shared, examined or used by a system, its boundaries matter. A word, a concept, a piece of software, a mathematical formula or a description of a feeling can require us to state exactly what we mean. An explanation then becomes something another person can examine, question and build upon.</p>
<p>Its foundations also need distinct roles. A <strong>premise</strong> is a working assumption adopted so reasoning or work can proceed while its truth remains open. An <strong>axiom</strong> is a foundational proposition about the world or domain taken as given within the model. An <strong>invariant</strong> states a condition the system must preserve. Definitions give the terms in these statements their meaning and boundaries; each statement then performs its own job.</p>
<p>An accepted definition is therefore a basic unit of <strong>authority over meaning</strong> in CyberAlchemy Lab. It establishes what a term is permitted to mean for a declared purpose and scope. That authority comes from an accountable owner’s acceptance of an exact version. The definition can then supply a criterion for an explanation, assessment or decision.</p>
<p>The Lab carries this into practical work by connecting definitions with saved records, rules, evidence and decisions. A <strong>record</strong> is an identifiable account of something the project needs to retain. A <strong>contract</strong> states requirements for a record or operation. Together, these let people and AI agents recover the meanings and conditions on which their work depends.</p>
</div>
<figure class="opening-image"><img src="assets/project-workbench-1280.webp" srcset="assets/project-workbench-640.webp 640w, assets/project-workbench-1280.webp 1280w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 40px), 720px" alt="An illustrated temporary workbench prepared from an orderly archive of project records, with a connected model and a checklist." width="1280" height="582" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption><span class="figure-number">Overview</span>A temporary workspace, supported by durable records. The diagrams below explain the model.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="part-heading">I · Governance</div>
<span id="lens-governance" class="anchor-point" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section class="article-section" id="governance" aria-labelledby="governance-title">
<div class="section-number">01</div><h2 id="governance-title">Three questions to keep in mind</h2>
<a class="map-section-link" href="#system-map" data-map-jump="governance">Explore this in the system map ↑</a>
<div class="prose"><p>Stating a meaning is the beginning. We also need to know who may establish that meaning, what supports the statements built on it and how it can change. <strong>Governance</strong> organizes these responsibilities and rules.</p><p>The Lab approaches them through three questions. <strong>Authority</strong> asks which accepted meanings and rules apply, who may act and within whose scope. <strong>Provenance</strong> asks where a record came from and how it was produced. <strong>Lifecycle</strong> asks which state it is in and which changes are permitted.</p><p>This chapter explains authority through the incident. Continue to <a href="#evidence">provenance and evidence</a>, then <a href="#change">lifecycle and admission</a>.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-governance-heading-2">An export with missing records</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>These questions remain connected while retaining their separate jobs. A definition can have accepted authority within its scope while evidence about the software using it remains incomplete. Permission to act and permission to put a change into use require their own records and decisions.</p><p>Let’s think of an agent investigating an incident involving a data extraction. A customer reports that records are missing from an export. The agent’s job is to assess that report and prepare evidence for the responsible owner.</p></div>
{{INCIDENT_CASE}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-governance-heading-3">What does “complete” mean here?</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>Before assessing the reported omission, the agent needs to recover the accepted meaning of “complete” for this export. The Lab would express that meaning through a definition like this:</p></div>
{{EXPORT_COMPLETENESS_DEFINITION}}
{{AUTHORITY_EXAMPLE}}
{{GOVERNANCE_AUTHORITY_ADDITIONS}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-governance-heading-4">What the comparison can establish</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The agent applies the criterion to the saved output from run {{CASE_RUN}}. It identifies the required rows from snapshot {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}}, for account {{CASE_ACCOUNT}} and filter <code>{{CASE_FILTERS}}</code>, then compares their identifiers with output {{CASE_OUTPUT}}. The export retrieved rows in successive batches, a technique called <strong>pagination</strong>; the criterion covers the entire required set.</p><p>The comparison confirms {{CASE_MISSING_COUNT}} missing required identifiers. This supports a bounded assessment of the reported failure. The agent still needs evidence to establish its cause and wider impact, and authority for any proposed operational change. The next section preserves the basis of that assessment.</p></div>
{{SOURCES_GOVERNANCE}}
</section>

<section class="article-section" id="evidence" aria-labelledby="evidence-title"><div class="section-number">02</div><h2 id="evidence-title">Show what the evidence actually supports</h2>
<a class="map-section-link" href="#system-map" data-map-jump="governance">Explore this in the system map ↑</a>
<div class="prose"><p>During the same incident investigation, the agent records the missing identifiers and the comparison that found them. A reviewer needs to recover the exact inputs, operation and responsible recorder. This is the job of <strong>provenance</strong>. In the Lab’s Definition format, its meaning can be presented like this:</p></div>
{{PROVENANCE_DEFINITION}}
{{PROVENANCE_EXAMPLE}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-evidence-heading-2">How the trail supports the claim</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The next agent or reviewer can recover the same snapshot, account, filters, output and comparison method. It can inspect the operation that produced the observation and check whether the assessment stays within those conditions. The owner’s acceptance record supplies the authority for definition {{CASE_DEFINITION_VERSION}}. Its source trail makes that acceptance inspectable.</p><p>Those sources become <strong>evidence</strong> for a particular claim through a declared relationship, with the subject version, conditions and freshness identified. The <strong>Evidence Set</strong> preserves the comparison, counterevidence and gaps. A <strong>Failure Assessment</strong> then records what is established about the occurrence, cause, impact, containment and recovery, retaining uncertainty in each. Here the omission is confirmed for output {{CASE_OUTPUT}}; the cause and recovery remain unestablished.</p></div>
{{FIGURE_EVIDENCE}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-evidence-heading-3">Keep the evidence records distinct</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A <strong>Measurement Method</strong> defines how to count and compare the rows. A <strong>Measurement Result</strong> preserves one execution and its conditions. The <strong>Operational Observation</strong> records what was seen during ordinary project work; the research-specific <strong>Observation</strong> belongs to research or experiment runs. A separate <strong>Finding</strong> derives from the Evidence Set and states the conclusion, limitations and challenge route. A Finding may also be supported by a Research Run under its applicable contract. These records let another reader inspect the reasoning without treating every record as the same fact.</p><p>A <strong>digest</strong> is a digital fingerprint of a file’s contents. It helps identify the exact file used as evidence. It does not establish that the file’s contents or the conclusion are correct.</p></div>
{{GOVERNANCE_PROVENANCE_ADDITIONS}}
<div class="case-note"><strong>What the agent can say</strong>“This export omitted rows that the comparison rule required. We have not yet established the cause.”</div>
{{SOURCES_EVIDENCE}}
</section>

<section class="article-section" id="change" aria-labelledby="change-title"><div class="section-number">03</div><h2 id="change-title">Make accepted changes explicit</h2>
<a class="map-section-link" href="#system-map" data-map-jump="governance">Explore this in the system map ↑</a>
<div class="prose"><p>At the incident’s next review checkpoint, the agent has preserved a comparison and prepared candidate assessment material. It may also propose a code change based on a hypothesis that still needs evaluation. The owner needs to distinguish what is accepted, what is proposed and what remains unresolved. <strong>Lifecycle</strong> organizes the states and change rules for each subject. The Lab’s lifecycle contracts can be explained through a definition in the same format:</p></div>
{{LIFECYCLE_DEFINITION}}
{{LIFECYCLE_EXAMPLE}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-change-heading-2">Authorize each requested effect</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>For software work using Craft, compilation produces selected artifacts and a receipt. Checks can provide evidence about an exact target. Recording those outputs in Project State is an attributable, governed action; acceptance and permission to put a revision into use still require their own decisions. A compiler receipt cannot establish that the Northstar repair goal is achieved or that the incident may close.</p></div>
<div class="prose"><p>An owner reviews the proposed change against its exact implementation contract, evidence and requested effects. Acceptance, adoption for use, activation, recovery evidence and incident closure have their respective requirements. Closing an incident preserves unresolved cause, remaining uncertainty and follow-up obligations.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-change-heading-3">Record the decisions and resulting state</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A <strong>Decision Bundle</strong> brings distinct decisions into one review while preserving their individual limits. The Lab calls formal acceptance <strong>admission</strong>. After authorization, an <strong>Admission Transaction</strong> applies the accepted records and their declared consequences together. It records the resulting <strong>Project State</strong>: the project’s saved records, definitions, evidence and decisions. One history preserves what changed; a separately linked history preserves why it was accepted. Every referenced record keeps its own standing. Saving a proposal in Project State does not itself accept that proposal.</p></div>
{{FIGURE_ADMISSION}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-change-heading-4">What a later agent can recover</h3>
{{GOVERNANCE_LIFECYCLE_CONTINUITY}}
{{INCIDENT_LENS_SUMMARY}}
<details class="facet-details" id="kinds-of-change"><summary>Three kinds of change—and why they matter</summary><p class="small">The Lab distinguishes a changed meaning, a changed encoding and a rebuilt view. These distinctions keep a format update from silently changing the rule it represents. A <strong>canonical record</strong> is the designated primary representation for its scope; a derived view is rebuilt from that record.</p><div class="facet-list"><div><strong>Semantic Epoch · changed meaning</strong><p>An owner-determined material change to meaning, scope or semantic requirements needs a successor meaning version and explicit owner selection. Changing which rows qualify for an export is one example.</p></div><div><strong>Representation Revision · changed format</strong><p>A new machine encoding preserves meaning with equivalence evidence, validation and owner selection. The earlier revision remains identifiable.</p></div><div><strong>Derived Refresh · rebuilt view</strong><p>Regenerating and validating a view from unchanged canonical input under an exact generator binding creates no new meaning or authority. A generator change is governed separately.</p></div></div></details>
<details class="facet-details"><summary>How someone can challenge an accepted record</summary><p class="small">A <strong>Challenge Contract</strong> states who may contest a revision, acceptable reasons and evidence, and who must respond. A <strong>Challenge Event</strong> records a particular challenge—for example, evidence contradicting an export-completeness finding. Filing it does not automatically suspend or withdraw the record. Those changes follow the contract and require an authorized decision.</p></details>
{{SOURCES_CHANGE}}
</section>

<div class="part-heading">Part II · The Lab model</div>
<div class="prose"><p>The three governance questions follow the work throughout its life. Now step back from the comparison and look at the records that organize the same investigation: why it matters, which product is affected, who coordinates the repair and what the agent is expected to do.</p></div>
<span id="lens-system" class="anchor-point" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section class="article-section" id="purpose" aria-labelledby="purpose-title"><div class="section-number">04</div><h2 id="purpose-title">Connect the customer need to the work</h2>
<a class="map-section-link" href="#system-map" data-map-jump="purpose">Explore this in the system map ↑</a>
<h3 class="article-subheading model-heading" id="docs-purpose-heading-2" data-model-concept="intent">{{INTENT_ICON}}<span>State the need before choosing the solution</span></h3>
<div class="prose"><p>An <strong>Intent</strong> states a problem, desired outcome or direction of change while the response is still being worked out. Northstar needs a complete eligible export. The report of missing rows gives that need urgency, but it has not yet established which code change would satisfy it.</p><p>An <strong>Intent Resolution</strong> records an accountable owner’s chosen response and satisfaction test. Here the owner chooses an export-repair project, beginning with an investigation and using the accepted identifier-coverage definition to assess completeness. That decision gives the effort a direction; success will need evidence.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading model-heading" id="docs-purpose-heading-3" data-model-concept="project">{{PROJECT_ICON}}<span>Keep the product and the repair effort identifiable</span></h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The <strong>Product</strong> is the durable identity that owns a promise of value: the customer platform that lets people manage and retrieve their data. The <strong>Project</strong> is the durable coordination identity for this particular effort: repairing Northstar’s export, with its goals, work, decisions and evidence. An <strong>Incident</strong> coordinates the response to the reported failure, including accountability, communications, recovery and closure.</p><p>These records answer different questions about the same situation. The platform can continue after this repair project finishes, and an investigation result can be recorded while the incident response remains open. The map pairs each general role with its place in this example.</p></div>
{{FIGURE_PURPOSE}}
<h3 class="article-subheading model-heading" id="docs-purpose-heading-4" data-model-concept="product">{{PRODUCT_ICON}}<span>Connect the promised outcome to the software</span></h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A <strong>Product Thesis</strong> explains why the platform is valuable and to whom. A <strong>Product Capability</strong> states an outcome that one product must enable for its consumers. Here that outcome is an export containing every required identifier within the declared account, filters and snapshot. An <strong>Implementation</strong> supplies the technical realization: service revision {{CASE_SERVICE_REVISION}} produced output {{CASE_OUTPUT}}.</p><p>A <strong>Capability Realization Binding</strong> records the association between an exact capability revision and exact implementation revisions, including their roles and adoption posture. Candidate service revision {{CASE_FIX_REVISION}} needs a successor binding revision if the selected endpoints or adoption posture change. The association, conformance evidence, adoption decision, deployment and observed operation remain separately recorded. The capability’s promised outcome gives the repair a criterion; its existence does not establish that the service meets it.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading model-heading" id="docs-purpose-heading-5" data-model-concept="work">{{WORK_ICON}}<span>Turn the target into bounded work</span></h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A <strong>Goal</strong> states a desired future outcome for one project and the conditions for declaring it achieved. The repair goal is to assess a replacement export with all required identifiers present. Its owner must review adequate evidence for all required success conditions and record achievement. The omission in {{CASE_OUTPUT}} is an investigation result, so the repair goal remains to be achieved.</p><p><strong>Work</strong> is a bounded unit of effort with a purpose, owner, participants, inputs, expected result and constraints. The agent’s current Work item is to investigate the omission. It can produce an observation and candidate assessment before the project has a proven repair. A <strong>Method</strong> supplies an approach, such as method {{CASE_METHOD_VERSION}}’s identifier comparison. A <strong>Workflow</strong> specifies ordered software Operations within or across Product Feature boundaries, with declared ownership scope. Workflow evaluation may produce a recovery intent and plan; invoking exact Operation bindings belongs to a separate executor. Further diagnosis, code changes and assessment use their own Work items and permissions.</p></div>
{{INCIDENT_WORK_BRIEF}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="craft-in-the-lab">Where Craft fits in this work</h3>
<div class="prose"><p><strong>Craft</strong> is the software-design Product in this broader Lab model. It provides a vocabulary for software responsibilities, architecture constraints and supported model-to-code construction. A project can use Craft’s tools while working on another Product. Here the customer-data platform owns the export promise, the repair Project coordinates the effort, and Craft could support a later software-change Work item.</p><p>The agent’s current assignment is still investigation. If the owner later authorizes implementation work, the agent could model the export criterion as a Craft <strong>Rule</strong>, declare the retrieval responsibilities and their connections, then select an architecture profile and supported implementation forms. Account, filters and snapshot must remain explicit. The resulting code and checks would supply outputs for the repair work; assessing the replacement export, adopting a revision, activating it and closing the incident would follow their respective requirements. This describes a possible use of Craft, not an executed repair.</p><p>A software <strong>Rule</strong> evaluates a condition; a <strong>Calculation</strong> derives a value from supplied information; a <strong>Query</strong> retrieves information; an <strong>Operation</strong> owns a business state change. Craft’s separate capacity example makes the distinction concrete: classifying 4 requested processing slots against a snapshot of 5 available slots returns Accept. Requests for 5 or 6 slots return Reject because the condition is strictly less than the available capacity. All three classifications leave the snapshot’s available capacity at 5. Local checks exercised these cases in the in-memory runtime and generated TypeScript, Python and Rust. A separate reservation Operation would check current capacity and reduce it to 1 while creating the reservation. A rejected reservation can leave state unchanged. These roles describe software behavior; accepting the repair evidence or authorizing deployment remains a Lab owner decision.</p><p><a href="../craft-documentation-proposal/craft-model.html#governance">Read how Craft fits into the Lab</a>, then follow its separate <a href="../craft-documentation-proposal/craft-model.html#definition">capacity-decision example</a> to see a definition become a typed software model.</p></div>
<details class="facet-details"><summary>How the Lab selects relevant information</summary><p class="small">A <strong>Coordination Context</strong> is a saved frame anchored to exactly one product. It may also identify one project and one primary goal, and records why information is needed and who will use it. A material change to those anchors or its purpose requires a successor context identity. A context profile supplies reusable selection rules; a request states what this task needs; a view contains the selected information.</p><p class="small">A <strong>Bounded Context</strong> defines meaning within one part of a domain. Billing and customer-data management, for example, may have different rules for what “active customer” means. A <strong>Context Map</strong> records how those models relate without assuming their terms are interchangeable.</p><p class="small">A <strong>Ledger</strong> is an ordered collection maintained under declared rules. Event histories have additional rules for preserving prior entries and reconstructing state from them.</p></details>
{{SOURCES_PURPOSE}}
</section>

<section class="article-section" id="records" aria-labelledby="records-title"><div class="section-number">05</div><h2 id="records-title">Separate meaning, shape and connections</h2>
<a class="map-section-link" href="#system-map" data-map-jump="ontology">Explore this in the system map ↑</a>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-records-heading-2">Give the investigation a meaning and a record format</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The Work brief above is readable because its fields have distinct jobs. A definition explains what <em>Work</em> means. An <strong>Artifact Type</strong> sets the required format for a Work record, and its <strong>schema</strong> specifies fields and constraints. The investigation Work item is an <strong>artifact instance</strong>: one identifiable, versioned record using that format. A real Lab record must resolve to the applicable contract; the brief illustrates its content for the reader.</p></div>
{{FIGURE_TYPES}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-records-heading-3">Select the governing records</h3>
<div class="prose"><p><strong>Registries</strong> are catalogs that identify the selected versions of definitions, types and connection rules. The Lab checks a record’s declared type against those rules. A familiar filename is insufficient to establish what the record is or whether it has been accepted.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-records-heading-4">Declare what each connection means</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A <strong>Relation Kind</strong> defines one kind of connection and the record kinds permitted at its source and target. In this investigation, the Operational Observation <em>originates from</em> the Source records for {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}} and {{CASE_OUTPUT}}. Their declared origin roles distinguish the required input from the observed output. A capability being a member of its product answers a different question and uses its own rule.</p><p>An <strong>ontology</strong> is a structured model of concepts, things and their relationships. An <strong>Ontology View</strong> selects part of that model for a purpose. The diagram shows the observation and sources first as records, then as nodes in a derived view of the same trail. Each node and edge must remain bound to the exact record versions it represents.</p></div>
<div class="diagram-tools" role="group" aria-label="Choose which graph layer to emphasize"><button type="button" data-graph="both" aria-pressed="true">Both layers</button><button type="button" data-graph="records" aria-pressed="false">Source records</button><button type="button" data-graph="view" aria-pressed="false">Graph view</button></div>
{{FIGURE_ONTOLOGY}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-records-heading-5">Extend rules and read views within their scope</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>Global connection rules declare a closed set of allowed source–target kind pairs. A proposed connection must fit an accepted rule and its exact pair requirements. Expanding that set requires review and admission of a successor rule contract; a different semantic question requires a distinct relation kind. The <strong>Typed Connection Extension Rule</strong> defines this process. Registering a new endpoint kind grants no additional connections.</p><p>A graph view must show which records and versions it represents, what it leaves out and whether it is still current. Following arrows can help find related evidence or permissions. The arrows themselves do not prove a claim or grant permission. Connections declared only inside a particular ontology remain governed by that ontology’s contract; they do not expand the global set.</p></div>
<details class="facet-details"><summary>How the Lab checks that concepts stay distinct</summary><p class="small">The Lab calls this <strong>non-collapse</strong>: checking whether concepts describe genuinely different facts. A task assignment can exist before permission to act is granted. A capability can be defined before an implementation is adopted. An experiment can evaluate a product and fail. These examples explain the distinctions; they are not formal proofs. The inspected formal-assurance report covers sources for 183 definition identities and partial translations into formal expressions. It reports zero verified proofs of whole definitions.</p></details>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="craft-and-lab-records">Keep software models and Lab records distinct</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A Craft model describes the software being built. The Lab’s records describe its meaning, the work around it, evidence and decisions. The same word can therefore appear at different levels. Each use needs its subject and contract.</p></div>
<div class="docs-comparison-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Lab records and Craft software responsibilities"><table class="docs-comparison"><caption>Related concepts with different responsibilities</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Lab responsibility</th><th scope="col">Craft responsibility</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Artifact Type</strong><br>Defines the required shape and obligations of a retained record.</td><td><strong>Meta-type</strong><br>Names a software role, such as Entity or Rule. It does not select the Lab record’s admission or lifecycle.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Work</strong><br>Identifies a bounded project effort, such as designing or evaluating a Workflow.</td><td><strong>Operation and Workflow</strong><br>Model a state-changing business action and orchestration of software Operations. The Lab’s admitted Workflow definition has this same software meaning; Work records the effort around it.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Capability Realization Binding</strong><br>Associates exact capability and implementation revisions with roles and adoption posture.</td><td><strong>Realization and artifact binding</strong><br>Select an implementation form and trace an output to its inputs. They do not establish adoption of a product capability.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Typed Relation</strong><br>Records a connection under a global Relation Kind and permitted endpoint pair.</td><td><strong>Software relationship</strong><br>Connects modeled concepts under Craft’s catalog and architecture constraints. A local verb does not expand the global Lab catalog.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Record lifecycle</strong><br>Governs the states and transitions of an exact definition, model or other record.</td><td><strong>State Machine</strong><br>Models a subject’s states inside the software’s domain. Its execution state does not describe the design’s acceptance.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<div class="prose"><p>A software-model node, the record retaining its declaration and a runtime object have different identities. One Entity concept can describe many running objects. An Ontology View can represent selected concepts and records with exact source bindings, while their local and global connections keep their own rules. A Craft feature organizes software responsibilities; establishing a Bounded Context still requires its own meaning, language, owner and context-map evidence.</p><p><a href="../craft-documentation-proposal/craft-model.html#governance-keep-the-two-kinds-of-model-identifiable">Compare the two models in the Craft article</a>. The correspondence between a governing Lab definition and a software Rule must be explicit and versioned under the applicable contract; sharing a name is insufficient.</p></div>
{{SOURCES_RECORDS}}
</section>

<span id="lens-agent" class="anchor-point" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section class="article-section" id="agent" aria-labelledby="agent-title"><div class="section-number">06</div><h2 id="agent-title" class="model-heading" data-model-concept="agent">{{AGENT_ICON}}<span>Give the agent a task, limits and a temporary session</span></h2>
<a class="map-section-link" href="#system-map" data-map-jump="agent">Explore this in the system map ↑</a>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-agent-heading-2">Establish the job and permissions</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The investigation Work item identifies the activity and expected result. An <strong>Agent Assignment</strong> binds the agent’s participation to that job, with its owner, scope, period and stop conditions. An <strong>Agent Profile</strong> describes reusable behavior, tools and stopping conditions. An <strong>Agent Capability</strong> describes an ability, such as comparing identifiers; the Product Capability states the useful outcome the platform must offer.</p><p>A <strong>Principal</strong> is the identity recognized for authorization or accountability at a particular system boundary. <strong>Delegation</strong> records permission from an authorized owner or principal for named actions within stated limits. For {{CASE_TICKET_ID}}, the service owner permits inspection of the saved material, identifier comparison, candidate recording and a change proposal during the 30-minute window. Changing source data, activating code and closing the incident have their own authority routes.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-agent-heading-3">Prepare a temporary session</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>An <strong>Agent Session</strong> brings one temporary machine-agent instance together with its assignment, views, policies, authority and working memory. For session {{CASE_SESSION}}, a <strong>Context View</strong> selects the governing definition, snapshot {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}}, output {{CASE_OUTPUT}}, account, filters and investigation records needed for the task. An Ontology View selects relevant concepts and connections, such as the observation’s source trail. Both identify their exact source versions and use conditions.</p><p>The Coordination Context introduced earlier frames this selection around one product, the repair project and its primary goal. That gives the agent a reason for receiving these records. The governing definitions, record contracts and current delegation determine how it may use them.</p></div>
{{FIGURE_AGENT}}
<div class="takeaway">Future sessions recover knowledge from saved Project State and its views. Working memory belongs to one session.</div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-agent-heading-4">Follow the same session through time</h3>
{{INCIDENT_SESSION_TIMELINE}}
<div class="memory-demo" role="group" data-theme="console" aria-label="Illustration of session memory and durable project state"><div class="memory-demo-head"><h3>What survives when the session ends?</h3><button type="button" class="ds-button" id="session-toggle">Close this session</button></div><div class="memory-columns"><div class="memory-column" data-model-concept="agent"><div class="memory-label">{{SESSION_MEMORY_ICON}}<span>Session Memory</span></div><p id="session-note">Working note: “Compare {{CASE_OUTPUT}} with the required identifiers for {{CASE_ACCOUNT}} in snapshot {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}}, keeping the filters explicit.”</p><p class="status" id="session-status">Temporary · current session only</p></div><div class="memory-column" data-model-concept="project"><div class="memory-label">{{PROJECT_STATE_ICON}}<span>Project State</span></div><p>Saved observation, evidence, candidate revision {{CASE_FIX_REVISION}}, decisions and histories.</p><p class="status">Saved outside the agent · available to later sessions</p></div></div><p class="small" id="session-explanation" aria-live="polite">This demonstration clears only the example note on this page. It does not close a real Lab session.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-agent-heading-5">Carry knowledge into the next session</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>When a machine-agent session ends, its instance ends and its working memory is cleared. Useful outputs are retained through the authorized recording route, with their own standing. The saved observation, candidate assessment and proposed revision {{CASE_FIX_REVISION}} can therefore survive without being treated as an accepted repair. A later instance starts with empty working memory and obtains new Context and Ontology Views of the saved Project State.</p><p>At the incident’s latest checkpoint, view {{CASE_VIEW_VERSION}} has expired. A new checked selection is needed before work resumes, along with current permission. Preparing that view preserves the earlier view’s history and does not extend the delegation window. This is the same separation between meaning, source trail, state and permission that the opening sections established.</p></div>
{{SOURCES_AGENT}}
</section>

<span id="lens-learning" class="anchor-point" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<section class="article-section" id="learning" aria-labelledby="learning-title"><div class="section-number">07</div><h2 id="learning-title">Turn experience into a lesson you can examine</h2>
<a class="map-section-link" href="#system-map" data-map-jump="learning">Explore this in the system map ↑</a>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-learning-heading-2">Keep the result and the hypothesis distinct</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The same investigation now supplies material for learning. An <strong>Operational Observation</strong> records the missing identifiers; an <strong>Agent Reflection</strong> proposes investigating batch boundaries under the declared snapshot conditions. The comparison has established the omission, while the location and cause still need evidence. <strong>Observation</strong> is the separate record kind used during a Research or Experiment Run.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-learning-heading-3">State when the advice applies</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>An <strong>Operational Lesson</strong> turns experience into candidate advice for future project work. It preserves its evidence, applicability, exclusions, predicted benefit and a <strong>falsifier</strong>: an observable result that would contradict the prediction.</p><p>A proposed lesson may turn out to be inapplicable to the incident that prompted it. Here the reflection leads to a candidate about checking adjacent batches when datasets can change between requests. Its stated exclusions include fixed-data exports with a different integrity contract, such as this assessment of {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}}. When reviewing that candidate for Northstar, a later agent can recognize the exclusion and record deliberate non-use. The proposed advice retains its place in Project State while this investigation still needs its own causal test and repair evidence. Saving a lesson does not make it a policy or permission.</p></div>
{{FIGURE_LEARNING}}
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-learning-heading-4">Separate the failure from its explanation</h3>
<div class="failure-comparison"><div><h3>Failure Mode · a possible pattern</h3><p>For example, changing row order between requests could cause rows to be skipped.</p></div><div><h3>Failure Occurrence · one episode</h3><p>This particular export has missing rows. Recording it does not yet establish the cause.</p></div><div><h3>Failure Assessment · the examination</h3><p>Investigate the pattern, cause, impact and recovery, while recording what remains uncertain.</p></div></div>
<div class="prose"><p><strong>Classification</strong> uses a declared scheme to organize failures and lessons—for example, by affected part of the system and observed impact. Classifying a failure does not establish its cause. An <strong>Incident</strong> coordinates a response when one is needed. A <strong>Failure Control Binding</strong> records which measure is intended to prevent a failure, detect it, limit its spread, restore operation or compensate for its effects.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-learning-heading-5">Test the cause within the incident’s scope</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>A <strong>Research Inquiry</strong> can ask whether batch retrieval omits required identifiers even when the source snapshot is fixed. An <strong>Experiment Design</strong> specifies the comparison, measurements and controlled conditions—for example, holding {{CASE_SNAPSHOT}}, {{CASE_ACCOUNT}} and <code>{{CASE_FILTERS}}</code> fixed while varying batch boundaries. An <strong>Experiment Run</strong> would record one execution. Such a run can evaluate a product or capability as well as address a research question.</p><p>That future work can assess revision {{CASE_FIX_REVISION}} against the same completeness criterion and preserve its findings within the tested conditions. Conformance evidence, an owner’s adoption decision, activation, recovery assessment and incident closure then follow their respective routes. The project goal requires its own evidence and recorded achievement.</p></div>
<div class="case-note"><strong>Why later use matters</strong>A later agent can see a lesson without using it, or use it without demonstrating benefit. A Lesson Application records actual use or deliberate non-use. A Lesson Outcome Assessment compares the predicted benefit with the observed result.</div>
{{SOURCES_LEARNING}}
</section>

<span id="lens-operation" class="anchor-point" aria-hidden="true"></span>
<div class="part-heading">Part III · The model and its current implementation</div>
<section class="article-section" id="today" aria-labelledby="today-title"><div class="section-number">08</div><h2 id="today-title">What the source snapshot establishes</h2>
<div class="prose"><p>The Northstar incident is a fictional case used to explain how the Lab organizes knowledge and work. The source snapshot inspected on 8 October 2026 also includes an accepted local runtime—the software that performs selected Lab operations on demand. Its accepted scope, recorded execution and readiness for regular use have their own evidence.</p></div>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-today-heading-2">Accepted records and remaining limits</h3>
<div class="status-board" role="group" aria-label="Recorded implementation and acceptance status">
<div class="status-row"><strong>Accepted project state <span class="state-chip">Recorded</span></strong><p>The saved Project State and its change and approval histories include acceptance of the local runtime. Future work can inspect what was accepted and why.</p></div>
<div class="status-row"><strong>Local runtime implementation <span class="state-chip">Admitted</span></strong><p>Provides local inspection, task-information preparation, authorized record changes, reconstruction from history and recovery within a tested scope. It uses a fixed selection of earlier accepted records. Later project changes do not update that selection automatically.</p></div>
<div class="status-row"><strong>Update to selected records <span class="state-chip candidate">Proposed</span></strong><p>Would make the runtime use the later accepted project state. Its recorded checks passed, but the update still requires separate acceptance.</p></div>
<div class="status-row"><strong>Ontology work</strong><p>There is an adopted code library for ontology lifecycles, a recorded past local execution and a migration prototype. Their documented scopes differ. They do not establish coverage of every current project object or a continuously running graph service.</p></div>
<div class="status-row"><strong>Stable release <span class="state-chip candidate">Not established</span></strong><p>Work remains on the product, installation, upgrades, recovery, publication and approval of an exact release.</p></div>
</div>
<p class="small">The local runtime’s admission excludes a general evaluator of organizational permissions, a continuously running service, external deployment and production durability. It records no runtime activation or external effect as part of that acceptance.</p>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="craft-integration-scope">Craft’s development and the Lab runtime</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The <a href="../craft-documentation-proposal/craft-model.html#craft-overview">Craft article</a> describes a product being developed for closed beta, including selected software contracts and a capacity fixture. Its active backend vocabulary, UI candidates and compiler support have their own scopes. They are separate from the local Lab runtime described above.</p><p>Using Craft in governed Lab work requires exact tool and implementation selections, an assignment with current permission, output-recording contracts and evidence for the performed actions. Linking the documentation does not establish an operational connector, automatic writes into Project State or continuous coverage of every object in an ontology. The intended fit is clear; each implementation claim still needs its own target and evidence.</p></div>
<p class="small">The proposed roadmap groups future work into these stages. They describe the intended progression; no completion percentage is implied.</p>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-today-heading-4">Work still ahead</h3>
<ol class="roadmap" aria-label="Proposed development stages"><li><span class="level" aria-hidden="true">01</span><strong>Foundation</strong><span class="posture">Demonstrate controlled local operation</span></li><li><span class="level" aria-hidden="true">02</span><strong>Repeatable operation</strong><span class="posture">Keep task information aligned with accepted changes</span></li><li><span class="level" aria-hidden="true">03</span><strong>Integrated work</strong><span class="posture">Connect engineering, research and learning</span></li><li><span class="level" aria-hidden="true">04</span><strong>Coherent ontology</strong><span class="posture">Build a consistent graph of the selected source material</span></li><li><span class="level" aria-hidden="true">05</span><strong>Usable product</strong><span class="posture">Establish a stable release with reliable recovery</span></li></ol>
<h3 class="article-subheading" id="docs-today-heading-5">Bring the whole investigation back together</h3>
<div class="prose"><p>The model keeps the customer’s need connected to the work and its consequences. Each stage has a distinct record, an accountable owner and a traceable basis. The three governance questions remain present throughout this loop.</p></div>
{{INCIDENT_SUMMARY_LOOP}}
<div class="prose"><p>At the current Northstar checkpoint, the omission is established, the candidate change still needs assessment and the agent is paused awaiting usable task information. A later session can recover those facts from saved Project State, resume within current permission and continue toward the repair goal. This is how a temporary agent participates in work whose knowledge, ownership and history continue beyond its session.</p></div>
{{SOURCES_TODAY}}
</section>
</article></div>

<span id="lens-reference" class="anchor-point" aria-hidden="true"></span>
